making the most of our water that is the  goal of the Colorado River water users  Association conference happening this  week at Caesar's Palace and the  association is a non-profit made up of  nearly a thousand members from seven  states sharing the Colorado River Mary  Jane beliesza explains the efforts  Southern Nevada is the most water  secured City in the desert Southwest  just in the last year the water district  tied water restrictions John Ensminger  is the general manager of Southern  Nevada Water Authority and a speaker at  this year's conference this is a river  that's in crisis everybody that lives  here can see the bathtub ring around  Lake Mead but you know some of the other  40 million Americans that were lap on  the river don't really realize how bad  things are out on the river Nathan Allen  is the executive director of water start  a local non-profit finding several  technological ways to extend water use  one of the examples of technology that  we're working with here in southern  Nevada is a technology that uses  artificial intelligence to in real time  plan out how water moves across the city  so you can make sure you keep the cost  of energy as low as you can while  maintaining the quality of the water  inside of the pipe those are benefits  that come back to the ratepayer and  having more safer cheaper water in the  city other Alternatives in the works  includes reducing evaporative cooling  like swamp coolers and finding  cost-effective ways to reduce that water  without raising your electric bill so  they build large cooling towers and that  water evaporates out into the air  doesn't get returned to Lake Mead so  that we can reuse it again and sminger  says this type of collaboration is  crucial to develop strategies and  address Water Management challenges this  is a situation we've been preparing for  for the last 20 years this isn't  sneaking up on us we have added 780 000  new residents to Southern Nevada since  2002 and we're using 26 percent of less  water than we were 20 years ago so we've  proven that you can grow your economy  you can add new people but you can also  use less water  to date Lake Mead levels have dropped  more than 170 feet since it was last  full that was back in 1983.
 

